WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:25.310
He’s one of 
the five longest reigning monarchs in the

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.120
history of the world.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.969
Emperor Franz Josef ruled over first Austria
and then Austria-Hungary for almost precisely

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:34.350
68 years.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.350
Coming to the throne on the back of a revolution
aged just 18, his reign coincided with some

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.730
of the most-important events in European history.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:48.760
It was while Franz Josef was emperor that
the Second French Empire rose and fell, that

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.729
Germany was forged by blood and iron, and
- most strikingly - that the events unfolded

00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:57.180
that would pave the way for WWI.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.339
Yet Franz Josef was more than just a mere
observer.

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:05.089
Under his watch, Vienna became a cultural
powerhouse, gifting the world Freud, Klimt,

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:06.759
Schiele, and Wittgenstein.

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:12.090
He oversaw the creation of one of the greatest
multinational empires in history… and then

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.560
lived long enough to sew the seeds of its
destruction.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:22.149
A cipher to many, a semi-mythological figure
to others, this is the life of Franz Josef,

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:25.689
Europe’s last great emperor.

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:32.500
Countdown to Revolution
If you were suddenly blasted back in time

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.580
to 1830, you would find yourself in a very
different world.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.460
Back then, there was no such thing as Italy.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.909
Germany was 39 weak states locked into an
alliance known as the German Confederation.

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:49.170
But the biggest difference you’d see would
be the great, round blob slap bang in the

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.400
middle of Europe.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.700
The blob covering not just northern Italy,
but modern-day Hungary, Czech Republic, Slovakia,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.469
Slovenia and Croatia, plus parts of Poland,
Ukraine, and Romania.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:05.109
Known as the Austrian Empire, it was this
imperial blob that Franz Josef was destined

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:06.109
to rule.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:10.670
Born on August 18, 1830, Franz Josef couldn’t
have come at a better time.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:16.000
His grandfather, Emperor Francis I, was nearing
the end of his life, and the line of succession

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.500
was far from clear.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.530
While Francis had two sons, the future emperor
Ferdinand I and Franz Josef’s own dad Francis

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.050
Charles, neither of them had so far produced
an heir.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.700
So when young Franz Josef emerged, kicking
and screaming into the light of day, the entire

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.440
Habsburg royal family gave a collective sigh
of relief.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.340
They finally had their future emperor!

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.820
Great though this was from a dynastic perspective,
it was less great from the point of view of

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.430
baby Franz Josef.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.030
From the moment he could talk, the young boy
was forced to stuff his brain with all the

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.430
knowledge a ruler could possibly need.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.500
This meant mastering military strategy.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.500
European history.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.480
It meant becoming fluent in not just German,
French and Latin, but also Austria’s minority

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.770
languages like Hungarian, Italian, Czech,
and Polish.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.760
Under the watchful eye of his ambitious mother,
Archduchess Sophie, young Franz Josef was

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.010
forced to spend nearly every waking moment
studying, his progress meticulously monitored

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.340
by Austria’s shadowy puppetmaster Metternich.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.990
As a result, the boy grew up to be both obsessed
with duty, and utterly lacking in imagination.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.010
For his part, Metternich considered this a
win.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.740
As far as he and Archduchess Sophie could
see, their precious heir was shaping up to

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.130
be the God-fearing military man they wanted
running the empire.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:39.810
Little did they know that empire would soon
be forced to fight for its very life.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.040
Revolution!

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.710
There’s a game you can play when your job
is producing history videos, and that game

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.000
is to see how far you can get into the biography
of any 19th Century figure before you have

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.060
to mention 1848.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.950
That’s because 1848 is the year Europe exploded.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:03.330
It started in February, when a French government
ban on banquets lit the spark on a massive

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.700
pile of dynamite marked “Decades of Public
Resentment”.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:12.180
The flames from the subsequent blast quickly
ignited another box of TNT marked “Decades

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.930
of Austrian Resentment,” which in turn ignited
another box marked “Hungarian Resentment,”

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.710
and another marked “Italian Resentment”,
and so-on.

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:22.560
It was a chain reaction of revolution.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.440
A series of wildfires that combined into a
European inferno.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.340
And it would nearly consume young Franz Josef.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:37.210
At the moment the sparks from France’s 1848
revolution landed on Vienna, Franz Josef was

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.370
a 17 year old lad, and his uncle Ferdinand
I was on the throne.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.230
The revolutionary fire that blew up on March
13 would change all that.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:51.270
That day, students excited by the news from
Paris gathered in Vienna to demand a new,

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.270
liberal constitution.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:57.000
In panic, Metternich ordered cavalry to attack
the crowd, triggering a riot.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.960
By 9pm that night, Austria’s puppetmaster
had resigned and fled the country.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.510
It was just the first in a series of shocks
that would bring Austria to its knees.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.210
In Hungary, the largest and most important
province of the Austrian Empire, liberals

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:17.610
forced Ferdinand I to grant Hungary near total
autonomy or face war.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.940
In Italy, they went one step further and actually
went to war.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.750
The news caused such unrest in Vienna that
the Habsburgs fled the city.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.580
But while most of the imperial family retreated
to safety, Franz Josef did the opposite.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:30.790
He went to Italy.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:35.020
That summer, Franz Josef personally fought
for Austria, turning himself into a soldier

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:36.020
hero.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:40.500
This was in great contrast to Emperor Ferdinand,
who quickly made himself into a villain.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.430
That fall, Ferdinand sent the imperial army
marching on Vienna.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.080
The breathtaking bloodshed that resulted allowed
the Habsburgs to retake their capital, but

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.690
it also left Ferdinand’s reputation in ruins.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:57.630
With Hungary now also at war with Austria,
it was decided the useless emperor had to

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:58.680
go.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.560
In December, 1848, Franz Josef’s mom, the
Archduchess Sophia, and an ultra-reactionary

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.130
known as Prince Felix zu Schwarzenberg staged
a palace coup.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.200
They forced Ferdinand to step down, replacing
him with Franz Josef.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.770
For the 18-year old boy, it must’ve been
a dizzying moment.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.320
Just six months earlier, he’d been a soldier,
fighting in Italy.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.100
And now here he was, ruler of an empire that
had self-destructed like a Humpty Dumpty made

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.860
of fissile plutonium.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:31.050
It was up to him to put that empire back together
again.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:37.980
Cracking Down
The arrival of Franz Josef on the throne was

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.710
initially treated with optimism by his subjects.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.490
He was a fresh-faced teenager.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.300
Surely he had to be more progressive than
the last guy?

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.580
Oh boy, were these 19th Century dudes in for
a rude awakening.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.360
Franz Josef’s main priority as emperor was
to end the war with Hungary.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.040
But he didn’t do this with negotiations.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.340
Instead, he called in the Russians.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.930
The Russian invasion both ensured that Hungary
would stay within the Austrian Empire, and

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:06.480
that the Hungarians would never trust Vienna
again.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.840
It didn’t help that Franz Josef followed
up Hungary’s defeat by having hundreds tried

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.620
for sedition, driven into exile or executed.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.050
When an unemployed tailor tried to assassinate
Franz Josef shortly after, it wasn’t the

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.949
emperor who ordinary Hungarians expressed
sympathy for, but his would-be assassin.

00:07:23.949 --> 00:07:28.810
Still, the crackdown Franz Josef launched
- coordinated from behind the scenes by Archduchess

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.590
Sophie and Prince Schwarzenberg - did have
the desired effect.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:37.610
Come the end of 1849, the fires of revolution
across Europe had been reduced to smouldering

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:38.610
embers.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:43.400
In France, the years of chaos had seen the
Second Republic rise and then fall as Napoleon’s

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.920
nephew, Napoleon III, seized power.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.870
In the German Confederation, the revolutionaries
had tried to unite all 39 states into a single

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:56.199
thing called “Germany”, only to implode
over the question of whether Austria or Prussia

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:57.199
should be its leader.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:02.000
But their efforts had at least brought one
Prussian farmer into politics for the first

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.000
time.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:08.120
Known as Otto von Bismarck, he and Franz Josef
were destined to collide with enough force

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.410
to reshape Europe.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:13.900
For now, though, no-one could tell just how
the failed revolutions of 1848 were going

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:14.930
to effect the future.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.190
In Austria, Franz Josef set about consolidating
his power.

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:23.970
Egged on by Archduchess Sophie and Prince
Schwarzenberg, he instituted a reign of neo-absolutism,

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:29.090
characterized by an absolute lack of freedom
of speech, a brutal secret police, and an

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.939
invasion of organized religion into every
aspect of citizens’ lives.

00:08:32.939 --> 00:08:38.329
Yet even at this frankly autocratic stage,
the young emperor did find it in his heart

00:08:38.329 --> 00:08:39.329
to do some good.

00:08:39.329 --> 00:08:44.519
Not long after taking power, he began dismantling
the anti-Semitic laws that had kept Austria’s

00:08:44.519 --> 00:08:47.540
Jews confined to ghettoes and locked out of
power.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:52.699
The destruction of these discriminatory statutes
would eventually result in a flourishing of

00:08:52.699 --> 00:08:54.399
Jewish culture in Vienna.

00:08:54.399 --> 00:08:58.649
But it’s doubtful Franz Josef was thinking
much about all this in the early 1850s.

00:08:58.649 --> 00:09:04.029
That’s because 1853 was the year the emperor
fell in love.

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:10.471
The Three Muses
In the story of Franz Josef’s life, there

00:09:10.471 --> 00:09:12.199
are three key figures.

00:09:12.199 --> 00:09:16.850
The first, obviously, was his mother, Archduchess
Sophie, who got him onto the throne.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:18.139
The second we’ve already met.

00:09:18.139 --> 00:09:23.660
Otto von Bismarck would shape the emperor’s
middle years, whether Franz Josef liked it

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:24.660
or not.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.560
The third was an unassuming Bavarian girl
of fifteen called Elisabeth.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.970
But you likely know her by her nickname: Sisi.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:37.100
A girl of jaw-dropping beauty with brown hair
that came down to her ankles, Sisi was the

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.449
sort of girl words like “radiant” are
reserved for.

00:09:40.449 --> 00:09:45.449
Franz Josef first met her in 1853, when he
traveled to Bavaria to propose to her older

00:09:45.449 --> 00:09:46.699
sister Helena.

00:09:46.699 --> 00:09:51.100
But the moment the 23 year old emperor got
his first look at Sisi, Helena metaphorically

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.540
went out the window.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:56.730
Instead, Franz Josef proposed to the girl
who’d just knocked him head over heels.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:01.149
The marriage between Franz Josef and Sisi
was controversial for a number of reasons.

00:10:01.149 --> 00:10:06.069
Back in Vienna, Archduchess Sophie was enraged
that her son had ditched safe and suitable

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:08.079
Helena for some teenage tramp.

00:10:08.079 --> 00:10:12.079
But it was also controversial in Bavaria,
for the simple reason that the free-spirited

00:10:12.079 --> 00:10:17.240
Sisi didn’t want to marry this boring emperor
any more than Sophie wanted her to.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:21.540
Nevertheless, Franz Josef and Sisi were married
on April 24, 1854.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.699
While the emperor really would spend the rest
of his life devoted to his new wife, it would

00:10:25.699 --> 00:10:27.629
not be a happy marriage.

00:10:27.629 --> 00:10:31.259
But if you want to hear more about that, you’ll
have to check out our Sisi video!

00:10:31.259 --> 00:10:33.619
Seriously, we’ve got a ton to get through
today.

00:10:33.619 --> 00:10:39.100
While we are gonna hear more about Sisi, we
don’t have time to do anything like justice

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:40.360
to her story.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.420
However, we will mention one salient fact.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:48.199
Unlike her husband, the Hungarophile Sisi
was very popular in Budapest.

00:10:48.199 --> 00:10:52.440
In fact, it’s probably their marriage that
did more than anything else to rehabilitate

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:53.920
Franz Josef’s image.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.069
The rest of the 1850s passed in a blur of
blunders and babies.

00:10:59.069 --> 00:11:05.420
Blunders, because 1855 saw Franz Josef alienate
both his ally Russia and the guys he wanted

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:11.449
to be his new BFFs, Britain and France, by
dithering over taking part in the Crimean

00:11:11.449 --> 00:11:12.449
War.

00:11:12.449 --> 00:11:14.899
And babies, because, well, Sisi had three
of them.

00:11:14.899 --> 00:11:18.350
The first, Sophie, sadly died during a family
visit to Hungary.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:23.110
The second, Gisela, sadly - from Franz Josef’s
perspective, at least - was a girl, and therefore

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:26.889
worthless in the ridiculous world of 19th
Century monarchs.

00:11:26.889 --> 00:11:29.749
The third, though, was when the emperor got
lucky.

00:11:29.749 --> 00:11:34.369
On April 21, 1558, Sisi gave birth to Crown
Prince Rudolf.

00:11:34.369 --> 00:11:38.850
For Vienna, the arrival of the emperor’s
new heir was cause for celebration.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.910
But while Franz Josef saw baby Rudolf as the
guarantee his line would continue, he couldn’t

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.179
have been aware of the darker truth.

00:11:46.179 --> 00:11:50.600
In just a few short decades, Crown Prince
Rudolf was going to drive the Austrian court

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.629
into deepest despair.

00:11:52.629 --> 00:12:00.690
Enter Bismarck
For all the 86 long years of his life, Franz

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:04.980
Josef would think of himself as a soldier
first and an emperor second.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:09.420
So it’s ironic that the lowest point in
his reign came thanks to his appalling military

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:10.420
skill.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.749
A year after Crown Prince Rudolf was born,
the Kingdom of Sardinia approached the flamboyant

00:12:14.749 --> 00:12:20.050
French dictator, Napoleon III, and suggested
teaming up to drive Austria out of Italy.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:25.980
To say the plot went to plan is to underestimate
just how blindly Franz Josef blundered into

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:26.980
the trap.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:32.019
In response to Sardinian aggression, he personally
rode out at the head of a vast army, only

00:12:32.019 --> 00:12:36.759
to watch in horror as the French appeared
on the horizon, blowing raspberries and telling

00:12:36.759 --> 00:12:40.350
him his mother was a hamster and his father
smelled of elderberries.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:45.819
The catastrophic loss of the 1859 war saw
Austria’s Italian possessions almost wiped

00:12:45.819 --> 00:12:46.819
out.

00:12:46.819 --> 00:12:50.769
Back home, Franz Josef was forced to call
an assembly to write a new, more liberal constitution

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:55.199
for the empire, lest the war’s outcome trigger
another 1848.

00:12:55.199 --> 00:12:57.560
Gone would be the secret police, and the iron
fist.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:02.279
In their place would be a new, cuddly Austria
that didn’t hate its own subjects.

00:13:02.279 --> 00:13:05.709
The trouble was, that was easier said than
done.

00:13:05.709 --> 00:13:10.100
Franz Josef was under tremendous pressure
to give concessions to the Hungarians, but

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:14.600
that would mean giving concessions to the
Poles and Czechs too, which would in turn

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.160
mean the Austrian right going into rebellion.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:23.040
It seemed all that nationalist feeling released
by 1848 hadn’t gone away.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.199
Keeping it from tearing apart the empire would
become a full time job.

00:13:27.199 --> 00:13:30.709
But not everyone thought nationalism was a
dangerous thing.

00:13:30.709 --> 00:13:35.670
While Franz Josef tried to keep the tide of
1848 from rising again, up in Prussia, Otto

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:41.339
von Bismarck was wily enough to know that
the only way to deal with a wave is by riding

00:13:41.339 --> 00:13:42.339
it.

00:13:42.339 --> 00:13:44.399
The 39 German states wanted to unite?

00:13:44.399 --> 00:13:46.839
Fine, he’d help them do it.

00:13:46.839 --> 00:13:48.540
But it would be on his terms.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:54.129
And that meant ensuring Austria had no part
to play in the coming Germany.

00:13:54.129 --> 00:13:57.480
And so we come to the Schleswig-Holstein Question.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.949
The details of what this actually was aren’t
important.

00:14:00.949 --> 00:14:04.260
Just know that Schleswig-Holstein were a pair
of states bordering Denmark, that they had

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:10.499
a question, and that in 1864 Bismarck answered
that question by punching Prussia’s iron

00:14:10.499 --> 00:14:13.429
fist right through the exam paper.

00:14:13.429 --> 00:14:17.480
Importantly for our story, he convinced Franz
Josef to help him do it.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:19.829
For Franz Josef it appeared an easy win.

00:14:19.829 --> 00:14:25.189
The joint invasion allowed Austria to occupy
Holstein and helped Vienna cosy up to Prussia.

00:14:25.189 --> 00:14:30.730
But Bismarck was playing the long game, the
one known as “Strengthen Prussia at Austria’s

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.730
Expense”.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:32.730
As always, Bismarck played to win.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:36.839
In January, 1866, Bismarck accused Austria
of misrule in Holstein.

00:14:36.839 --> 00:14:40.920
Before long, the Iron Chancellor had whipped
up tensions so skilfully that Franz Josef

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.509
was forced to declare war.

00:14:42.509 --> 00:14:46.679
The Seven Weeks War was as short and one-sided
as its name suggests.

00:14:46.679 --> 00:14:52.279
Devoid of allies, Franz Josef could do nothing
but watch as the highly trained Prussian Army

00:14:52.279 --> 00:14:53.870
steamrollered Austria.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:59.600
The defeat was so colossal that Prussia was
able to force Austria out of the united Germany

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.549
Bismarck was now actively building.

00:15:01.549 --> 00:15:05.869
In fact, the only reason the Seven Weeks War
didn’t end with the Austrian Empire disintegrating

00:15:05.869 --> 00:15:10.050
is because Bismarck didn’t want to deal
with a failing state on his doorstep.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.499
But the damage had been done.

00:15:12.499 --> 00:15:16.790
Austria was now weak, and Franz Josef’s
reputation was in tatters.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.889
It would take a miracle to hold everything
together after this.

00:15:19.889 --> 00:15:21.059
A miracle… or a Compromise.

00:15:21.059 --> 00:15:29.290
The Dual Monarchy
For

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:33.749
the Hungarians, watching Austria get kicked
around by Bismarck was the last straw.

00:15:33.749 --> 00:15:38.589
In late 1866, they basically told Vienna “look,
we’re doing this independence thing.”

00:15:38.589 --> 00:15:39.910
In Vienna, panic descended.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:44.670
Some seriously counseled going to war with
the Hungarians, consequences be damned.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:48.160
But Franz Josef was through being a loser.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.609
Ignoring his conservative advisors, he instead
looked to his own wife, Sisi, a long time

00:15:53.609 --> 00:15:54.609
Hungarophile.

00:15:54.609 --> 00:15:59.509
With her support, and that of the court liberals
who surrounded her, Franz Josef managed to

00:15:59.509 --> 00:16:02.759
get the hawks to abandon their war plans.

00:16:02.759 --> 00:16:06.420
In place of those plans, Vienna and Budapest
negotiated a compromise.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:11.269
The Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867 was
a masterpiece of diplomatic footwork.

00:16:11.269 --> 00:16:16.630
It split the Austrian Empire into two separate
states: Austria (also called Cisleithania)

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:19.379
and Hungary (also called Transleithania).

00:16:19.379 --> 00:16:24.129
They each had their own constitutions, their
own systems of government, and their own monarchical

00:16:24.129 --> 00:16:25.129
structures.

00:16:25.129 --> 00:16:28.799
The key was that the Austrian Emperor and
the Hungarian King would henceforth be the

00:16:28.799 --> 00:16:31.189
same person: Franz Josef.

00:16:31.189 --> 00:16:35.600
Joint ministries would also govern defense,
foreign affairs, and finance.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:39.699
It was tiptoeing to the edges of independence,
but not quite jumping.

00:16:39.699 --> 00:16:43.129
From this, the Austro-Hungarian Empire was
born.

00:16:43.129 --> 00:16:48.399
For Franz Josef, this meant not just saving
his empire from near-certain collapse, but

00:16:48.399 --> 00:16:51.579
being crowned King of Hungary in a lavish
ceremony.

00:16:51.579 --> 00:16:55.350
Barely twenty years earlier, he’d been a
warrior emperor, demanding the insurrectionist

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.679
Hungarians be hanged for treason.

00:16:57.679 --> 00:17:03.920
Now, here he was, sat alongside his Hungarophile
wife, accepting the elevation of Hungary to

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.470
partner in empire.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.819
To his credit, Franz Josef accepted the world
had changed.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:14.480
That December, 1867, he gave Austria the constitution
it had been begging for since 1848.

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:17.709
The new constitution laid out citizens’
fundamental rights.

00:17:17.709 --> 00:17:20.360
It established Austria’s first supreme court.

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:23.120
It guaranteed the independence of the judiciary.

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:24.520
The message was clear.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:30.380
The Austro-Hungarian Empire was a different,
more liberal beast to the Austrian Empire.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:33.760
However, we shouldn’t kid ourselves that
everything was cool.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:38.650
Although the new constitution placed great
emphasis on rule via elected representatives,

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:43.190
it maintained a veto that Franz Josef could
use on any law.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.180
And he did, a lot.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.610
There was also still the national question.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:52.260
When the Hungarians were elevated to equals,
the Czechs were understandably all like “yeah,

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:55.210
how come we don’t get any of that action?”.

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.669
Over on the Hungarian side, things were even
more vexed.

00:17:57.669 --> 00:18:03.050
While the “Austro” part of Austro-Hungary
gave equal language rights to its minorities,

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:06.799
the Hungarians were all about stuff being
Hungarian.

00:18:06.799 --> 00:18:10.700
That meant Romanians, Slovenes and Serbs all
having to learn the language.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.429
Still, the new empire was broadly a success.

00:18:13.429 --> 00:18:18.799
As Bismarck forged the German Empire from
blood and iron, here was Austro-Hungary, rising

00:18:18.799 --> 00:18:20.470
instead from compromise.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:26.110
It was this iteration of the empire that would
give the world the grandeur of Imperial Vienna;

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:30.299
the secession movement of architecture; and
the founding of psychoanalysis.

00:18:30.299 --> 00:18:50.380
Unfortunately for Franz Josef, this golden
age would also coincide with the darkest period

00:18:50.380 --> 00:19:02.260
of

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:08.950
his life.

00:19:08.950 --> 00:20:07.330
Goodbye, My Love
What’s remarkable with hindsight is that

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:12.389
we can see the storm clouds gathering over
both Austro-Hungary and Franz Josef even during

00:20:12.389 --> 00:20:13.389
the good times.

00:20:13.389 --> 00:20:18.100
On the empire side, there’s the occupation
of Bosnia by Austro-Hungarian troops in 1878,

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.100
that sparked Serbian fury.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:24.910
There’s also the treaty Franz Josef drew
up with Otto von Bismarck in 1879.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:29.450
The one that stipulated the two states would
fight on each other’s side in any European

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:30.450
war.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.759
But it was the personal side of things that
likely hit Franz Josef hardest.

00:20:33.759 --> 00:20:38.419
It started in 1872, when his mother, Archduchess
Sophie, finally passed away.

00:20:38.419 --> 00:20:43.100
But it really took off in 1881, when young
Crown Prince Rudolf – remember him?

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:44.890
– finally married Princess Stephanie of
Belgium.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:48.410
You’re probably thinking something like
“well that doesn’t sound so tragic,”

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:50.350
but sadly it was.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:55.090
Rudolf was an introverted, melancholy lad
who didn’t want to marry this boring princess.

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:59.840
He took to having affairs, sliding deeper
into unhappiness as he cut himself off from

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:01.100
the outside world.

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.370
It was in this state that he met Baroness
Mary.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:08.880
A 17-year old romantic, Mary was drawn to
the gloomy, melancholic side of Rudolf.

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:13.779
The two began an affair in which they guided
one another ever further into heartache and

00:21:13.779 --> 00:21:14.779
depression.

00:21:14.779 --> 00:21:18.909
Finally, on January 30, 1889, the pair retreated
to Rudolf’s hunting lodge.

00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:24.299
There they made a suicide pact before Rudolf
shot Mary dead and turned the gun on himself.

00:21:24.299 --> 00:21:28.620
And, just like that, Franz Josef’s only
male heir was gone.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.980
The shock of the incident destroyed the imperial
family.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:36.570
Never happy in Vienna, Sisi now fled the city,
casting herself out into the world, wanting

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.259
only to be reunited with her dead son.

00:21:39.259 --> 00:21:43.000
She got her wish just nine years later, when
an anarchist stabbed her to death while she

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:44.539
was out walking in Geneva.

00:21:44.539 --> 00:21:47.730
With her death, Franz Josef was suddenly alone.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:51.490
By now a man in his late 60s, the emperor
had reached a level of popularity with his

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:54.530
subjects his younger self couldn’t have
dreamed of.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.110
In the imperial court, though, it was another
story.

00:21:57.110 --> 00:22:01.659
With Rudolf gone, Franz Josef had been forced
to make his nephew, Franz Ferdinand, his heir.

00:22:01.659 --> 00:22:06.970
This was a problem, as Franz Ferdinand was
pretty much the most unpopular man in Austria.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:09.830
Everyone who met him invariably wound up hating
him.

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:14.070
He was pompous, stuffy, awkward, angry, and
only happy when he was out hunting.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:18.220
Yet, there’s an argument to be made that
Franz Josef couldn’t have picked a better

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:19.220
heir.

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:20.620
Franz Ferdinand was a dick.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:23.350
But he was also shrewd.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:26.399
He could see that the empire’s future lay
in more federalism.

00:22:26.399 --> 00:22:31.370
That he could shore up support by elevating
the Czechs and Serbians to joint equals with

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:33.269
the Austrians and Hungarians.

00:22:33.269 --> 00:22:37.820
There’s even a school of thought that, had
Franz Josef been assassinated instead of Sisi,

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:43.929
and Franz Ferdinand taken his place, there
might even still be an Austro-Hungary today.

00:22:43.929 --> 00:22:45.880
But that’s not what happened.

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.731
For all his bright ideas, Franz Ferdinand
would not go down in history as the man who

00:22:49.731 --> 00:22:50.850
saved the empire.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:54.950
Rather, he would go down in history as the
man whose death killed millions.

00:22:54.950 --> 00:23:03.240
The End of it All
By the dawn of the 20th Century, decades of

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.140
heartbreak had taken their toll on Franz Josef.

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:10.990
The emperor had withdrawn from politics, settling
into his role as a figurehead of empire.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.590
This would’ve been great if he actually
was a figurehead.

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:16.419
But he was an integral part of the system.

00:23:16.419 --> 00:23:20.700
Without Franz Josef’s input, the empire
began a period of listless drifting known

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:22.210
as Fortwusteln.

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:26.020
While culture continued to thrive in Vienna,
the only thing of significance to really happen

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:31.530
before the 1910s was when Austria annexed
Bosnia in 1908, kicking off a crisis that

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:32.659
nearly led to war.

00:23:32.659 --> 00:23:36.760
But while war was avoided in 1908, it wouldn’t
be kept at bay for much longer.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:41.799
On June 28, 1914, the empire’s heir, Franz
Ferdinand, was visiting the newly-annexed

00:23:41.799 --> 00:23:46.029
province of Bosnia when a Bosnian-Serb terrorist
shot him dead.

00:23:46.029 --> 00:23:48.850
The assassination sent shockwaves through
Vienna.

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:53.020
When evidence emerged that the Serbian intelligence
services may have had a black hand in the

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:55.330
killing, Franz Josef was forced to act.

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:59.929
At the advice of the hawks in his court, the
emperor drew up an ultimatum to Serbia that

00:23:59.929 --> 00:24:02.100
was effectively impossible to follow.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:06.460
In response, Russia and France gleefully announced
they would defend Serbian honor from this

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:07.460
Austrian aggression.

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:10.630
Which made Germany declare they’d defend
Austrian honor.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.179
Which made Britain…

00:24:12.179 --> 00:24:15.580
Well, you know the rest.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:20.610
On July 28, 1914, Franz Josef signed the decree
declaring war on Serbia.

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:23.419
It would be the last major act he’d undertake
as emperor.

00:24:23.419 --> 00:24:28.450
The declaration effectively started WWI, not
that we should blame something so complex

00:24:28.450 --> 00:24:30.279
on poor old Franz Josef.

00:24:30.279 --> 00:24:34.960
At hundreds of points, somebody in Serbia,
or Russia, or France, or Germany, or Britain

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:37.240
could’ve blinked and averted catastrophe.

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:41.610
That they didn’t was a failing that went
beyond one doddering old emperor.

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:45.460
Although Franz Josef didn’t live to see
it, the declaration would kill his beloved

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:46.730
empire.

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:50.900
In 1918, as the war drew to a close, the many
nationalities of Austro-Hungary all made a

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:53.540
break for it, shattering the empire for good.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:56.820
But, by this point, Franz Josef was no longer
around.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:01.960
On November 21, 1916, the elderly emperor
had died of pneumonia, passing away in the

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.700
same palace he’d been born in.

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:08.690
His funeral procession on November 30 would
be the last time his empire came together

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:09.809
in unity.

00:25:09.809 --> 00:25:13.429
Even at the time, people felt it was the end
of an era.

00:25:13.429 --> 00:25:15.649
Two years later, it would all be gone.

00:25:15.649 --> 00:25:20.840
From our vantage point of the 21st Century,
we can see that Franz Josef was a flawed man.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:25.260
While he instituted reforms that turned the
autocratic Austrian Empire into a powerhouse

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:28.700
of culture, he only did so when his hand was
forced.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:33.700
Equally, while he was a decent general, we
can also see that he was hopeless when faced

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:37.110
with a great strategic mind like that of Otto
von Bismarck.

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:40.890
What do we mean, then, when we call him the
Last Great Emperor?

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:43.780
Well, there are different ways of defining
great.

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:47.519
For some, that means a person who is exceptional,
someone like Napoleon.

00:25:47.519 --> 00:25:52.259
But it can also mean someone who embodies
a certain way of being.

00:25:52.259 --> 00:25:56.840
Franz Josef grew up in a world where emperors
were father figures who looked after their

00:25:56.840 --> 00:26:01.880
subjects, neither the tyrants of yesterday
nor the mere symbols of today.

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:06.299
By sheer dint of his long life, he became
one of the very last of this type, a widely

00:26:06.299 --> 00:26:11.850
beloved figure who managed to unite multiple
nationalities under his rule, even as he still

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:13.720
took an active hand in empire.

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:18.789
He may have been unimaginative, obsessed with
duty, and too slow to recognize the need for

00:26:18.789 --> 00:26:19.789
change.

00:26:19.789 --> 00:26:24.230
But Franz Josef was likely the last great
emperor Europe will ever see.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:54.100
With his death, an entire age was lost forever.

